ATTACK 1 — end-to-end pair_key stability across two independent loads
(get_suspected_transfers' load, then resolve_transfer's load). Read
internal/services/transfers/transfers.go: PairKeyFor(idA, idB) is
sha256(lo|hi) truncated to 12 hex chars, a pure function of the two legs'
Identity() (StableID, or fallback). internal/models/transaction.go:
StableIDFor(accountID, date, amountCents, occurrence) is itself a pure
function of content, and dataloader/stable_id.go assigns it deterministically
from parsed CSV rows on every load. So two loads of the SAME underlying CSV
data produce the same StableIDs, hence the same PairKeyFor, hence the same
pair_key -- consistent with resolve.go's own pre-existing re-validation
comment ("SuspectedTransfers() only reflects the most recent load ... after
the CSVs change the cached queue would be stale") already covering the case
where data DOES change between calls (both the mint and the write-time
re-check call deps.load() fresh and re-check suspectedContains, so a changed
key after a real data change is refused, not silently accepted). This is
pre-existing behavior this task rides on, not a new risk it introduces.
No dead-end and no undocumented invalidation window found.

